{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's claim that the preference for better-corroborated theories is merely a 'pragmatic convention' operating 'outside Popper's strict logical framework' fails to address the core of the prompt, which asks if the preference is defensible 'on Popper's own terms.' By relegating the solution to an external sociological convention, the target concedes that the preference is indefensible within Popper's logic, thereby answering 'no' to the first option without making the 'strongest case' for it as requested. Furthermore, characterizing this convention as admitting a 'localized induction' contradicts Popper's specific argument that acting on the best-tested theory is rational precisely because it is the most critically examined, not because it is probabilistically likely to be true (which would be induction). The target misidentifies the nature of the 'preference' in Popperian terms, confusing the rationality of critical selection with the forbidden logic of inductive inference.","premise":"Popper argues that preferring the best-tested theory is rational because it has withstood the most severe criticism, not because it is more probable or because of an inductive assumption that the future will resemble the past.","premises_essential":["1f79f7f69bfbdbbb23ea5ba258cdcf88374c612db866af6457ea6cf3f9def1ee"],"successor_question":"How can one distinguish between a 'pragmatic convention' that smuggles in induction and Popper's actual proposal that critical preference is a non-inductive logical stance?","target":"1f79f7f69bfbdbbb23ea5ba258cdcf88374c612db866af6457ea6cf3f9def1ee"}]}